10:
Omniscience is called 'unobstructed knowledge' (anāvaraṇa-ñāṇa) because it proceeds without attachment or hindrance toward its objects. However, for the purpose of showing its unique (asādhāraṇa) nature, which is not shared with other Paccekabuddhas and Noble Disciples, it is divided into two and mentioned within the classification of the Six Uncommon Knowledges (cha-asādhāraṇa-ñāṇa).

In this introductory section, for what reason is only the destruction of ignorance (avijjā-samugghāta) taken for the praise of the Blessed One by way of the perfection of abandonment (pahāna-sampadā)? Why is the abandonment of all defilements without remainder not taken? It is said: By the very mention of abandoning that ignorance, the destruction of all defilements (saṅkilesa) that coexist with it in a single unwholesome thought (or person) is illuminated; therefore, the abandonment of all defilements without remainder is included here. For there is no defilement that is not abandoned by the total uprooting of ignorance without remainder.

Alternatively, just as wisdom is the primary cause for the arising of all wholesome states and for the cessation of the round of rebirth (saṃsāra), so too ignorance is the primary cause for the arising of all unwholesome states without remainder and for the continuation of the round of rebirth. Therefore, by the mere mention of destroying that ignorance, the destruction of all defilements is established, and thus only that one is taken. Alternatively, because ignorance is the 'head' of all defiling states, its destruction alone is taken. As the Blessed One said:
'Know ignorance as the head;
Wisdom, joined with faith, mindfulness, concentration,
Desire, and energy,
Is the cutter of the head.'

In the term 'sanarāmaralokagaru', according to the first primary sense, a being is called 'nara' without distinction. Here, however, according to the second sense, it refers only to a human male; otherwise, the subsequent word 'loka' would be redundant. As stated in the Nāvāvimāna Commentary: 'Just as a being in the first sense is called "purisa" because he dwells in a high position due to the sense of excellence, so too he is called "nara" because he leads to a state of greatness. Even a person who is a son or a brother stands in the place of a father to his mother, elder sister, or younger sister; how much more so a husband to others.' The word 'narā' is formed either by 'ekasesa' or as a plural derivative. Death is called 'mara'; those who do not have it are 'amara' (devas). The world together with humans (nara) and devas (amara) is 'sanarāmaraloka'. He is 'garu' because He is eminent and famous; for the word 'gara' exists in the sense of rising or becoming manifest. Furthermore, He is called 'garu' in the sense of being 'heavy' (venerable), like a stone umbrella.

The word 'garu' exists in the sense of parents and teachers;
In things that are difficult to destroy;
In objects that are not light (heavy);
In things that are great;
In fame;
And in subtle skill and the performance of respect.
